  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Families Enjoy Improved Health Through Access to Safe Water</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Vitichi, a rural municipality in Potosi, is considered a red area for poverty nationwide. The population of this area practices extensive animal husbandry and subsistence agriculture, yet they severely struggle due to a historic water shortage that, over the last 10 years, has been exacerbated by changes in rainfall patterns which are caused by global climate change. 

55% of the inhabitants (11,000 people) of this municipality are women whose situation and vulnerability are aggravated by high levels of illiteracy and because they have less chances of migrating than men. To these families, electricity, potable water and sewer systems are unimaginable luxuries; significantly increasing the risk of diarrhoea, skin and other diseases.  

As part of the Programme seeking to address issues of water contamination and scarcity in the Altoplano, ACLO Potosi will target 214 impoverished, indigenous households in 7 communities in 3 districts (Ara, Calcha and Vitichi), improving their health by increasing their access to drinking water and increasing their income by improving the conditions in which they raise their cattle (mainly goats and cows). 

ACLO Potosi is experienced in advocacy and leadership capacity strengthening, and therefore they will also work with grassroots organizations, community leaders and the municipal government to influence regional and national legislation and development policies.</narrative>
  </description>
